    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you go more into detail on the enzyme part specifically? like how exactly does the lisinopril interact with the ACE enzyme to stop the conversion? i want to be able to explain the binding process if they ask.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Lisinopril acts primarily at the active site of the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). It binds covalently to the zinc ion present at this site via a carbonyl group on the lisinopril molecule. This interaction results in the acylation of a serine residue on ACE, thereby modifying its activity and effectively inactivating it. This halted activity prevents angiotensin I from being converted into angiotensin II. The modified enzyme is later removed by proteases from the secretory pathway. This irreversible binding is the key to lisinopril's sustained inhibition effect, as it ensures that each molecule of ACE has a permanent effect, contributing to the drug's ability to reduce blood pressure consistently.",
      "source": "target",
